B.FE=s(["Flutter","Architecture","Clean Code"],t.s)
B.z_=new A.m8("Architecting a Flutter app for the long haul","How layering, dependency inversion and a single source of truth for design tokens kept a three-year-old codebase healthy enough to ship new features in days, not weeks.","Most Flutter apps start as a single main.dart and grow organically from there. That works beautifully for a hackathon, but by the time you have a dozen features, shared design system and a team of engineers touching the same files, the cracks show quickly.\n\nI have landed on a feature-first folder structure with a thin shared core. Each feature owns its domain models, seeded data and UI, while cross-cutting concerns like theming, responsive breakpoints and configuration live under lib/core.\n\nThe single biggest lever is dependency inversion. When widgets depend on abstractions instead of concrete services, you can swap a Firebase backend for a Supabase one without touching a single screen. It also makes the codebase testable in a way that god-objects never are.\n\nFinally, treat design tokens as code. A single abstract final class holding every colour, radius and spacing value means a rebrand is a one-file change instead of a find-and-replace marathon across hundreds of widgets.",B.FE,"assets/blog/architecture.jpg","2026-07-02")
B.Fz=s(["Flutter","Riverpod","BLoC","State"],t.s)
B.z0=new A.m8("Riverpod vs BLoC: a pragmatic comparison","Both solve state management. Neither is universally better. Here is how I choose between them on a new engagement, with real trade-offs around boilerplate, testability and team size.","The Flutter community loves a state management holy war, but the honest answer is that Riverpod and BLoC both produce maintainable apps when used consistently. The decision should be driven by your team, not by internet points.\n\nRiverpod shines when you want compile-safe dependency injection with minimal boilerplate. Providers compose naturally and the consumer widgets stay tiny. I reach for it on smaller teams and greenfield apps where the team is comfortable with reactive patterns.\n\nBLoC pays off on larger teams that need strict separation between events, states and presentation. The ceremony is real, but it forces every state transition through a single auditable path, which is worth its weight in gold when a QA team needs to reproduce a bug from a log.\n\nMy rule of thumb: pick the one your senior engineers can explain to a junior in ten minutes. Consistency beats cleverness every time.",B.Fz,"assets/blog/state.jpg","2026-06-18")
B.FA=s(["Flutter","Web","Responsive"],t.s)
B.yY=new A.m8("Shipping a Flutter web app that actually feels native","Web is a first-class target, not an afterthought. From URL routing to pointer ergonomics, these are the details that separate a Flutter web app from a mobile app stretched onto a desktop browser.","Flutter on the web gets a bad reputation, mostly from apps that ship mobile layouts to desktop viewports and call it a day. With a little care, a Flutter web app can feel indistinguishable from a hand-rolled React site.\n\nStart with responsive breakpoints and respect them everywhere. A 12-column grid at 1440px, an 8-column grid on tablets, and a single column under 768px gives every viewport a layout that feels intentional.\n\nNext, embrace declarative routing. A URL is a contract with your user: it should be shareable, bookmarkable and survive a refresh. go_router makes this almost free, and it pays off the first time someone deep-links into your app.\n\nFinally, handle pointer devices explicitly. Mouse hover states, scroll wheel capture and keyboard shortcuts are the difference between a web page and a web application. Treat them as features, not afterthoughts.",B.FA,"assets/blog/web.jpg","2026-05-30")
B.Fx=s(["Design","Tokens","Flutter"],t.s)
B.yZ=new A.m8("Design tokens: one file to rebrand them all","Why hardcoding colours and radii in widgets is technical debt, and how a single abstract final token class turns a rebrand from a multi-day sprint into a one-file change.","Every design system eventually needs to be expressed in code, and the question is always where those values live. The worst place is inline in widgets, scattered across hundreds of files. The best place is a single, well-documented source of truth.\n\nI model design tokens as an abstract final class with const fields. Colours, typography scale, spacing grid, shape radii and elevation values all live there. Widgets reference tokens by name, never by raw hex value.\n\nThe payoff is enormous. When a brand changes its primary colour, you change one constant and the entire app updates. When a designer wants to experiment with a 6px radius instead of 8px, it is a one-line change. And because the tokens are const, there is zero runtime cost.",B.Fx,"assets/blog/tokens.jpg","2026-05-12")
B.GK=s([B.z_,B.z0,B.yY,B.yZ],A.ad("w<m8>"))
